# AGENTS.md — demo hub

Hub for the demo workspace. Project values (repos, tracker team, branch prefix, checks, guard paths) live
only in `hub.json`; read it instead of assuming names. Each repo has its own `AGENTS.md`; read the one for the repo you
change. This project's own rules are in `AGENTS.project.md`.

**Day 0 rule:** every change to a repo goes through this hub and the workflow below. If the hub gets in the way, that
is a hub bug: log it in `brain/_inbox/`.

## What lives here

- `hub.json`: the project config, checked against `hub.schema.json`. `hub.lock` records what `hub sync` last wrote.
- `Makefile`: workspace entry points (`make help`). They run the `hub` release pinned in `hub.json` →
  `platform.version`. Project targets go in `Makefile.project`.
- `brain/`: curated, versioned project memory. Start at `brain/index.md`; `brain/now.md` is the current focus. Read on
  demand, never in bulk.
- `hub sync` rewrites these managed files: `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `Makefile`, `hub`, `agent`,
  `hub.schema.json`, `.claude/settings.json`, `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, `.pre-commit-config.yaml`,
  `plugin/hub-workflow/`, `scripts/mine_transcripts.py`, `scripts/recall_transcripts.py`, `scripts/retro_metrics.py`,
  and each selected module's files (`mk/<id>.mk`, `scripts/cloud-setup.sh`).
  Change them only through `hub.json` or their seeded sibling (`AGENTS.project.md`, `Makefile.project`,
  `.claude/settings.project.json`, `plugin/demo/`), which `hub sync` never touches.
- `.claude/settings.json` already runs the `hub-workflow` hooks, so that plugin must not be enabled from a marketplace
  in this hub: Claude Code drops only identical duplicate hook commands, so every hook would run twice.
- `/feature` and `/create-plan` use superpowers skills when that plugin is enabled, and a one-line fallback otherwise.
  To enable it, add `"enabledPlugins": {"superpowers@claude-plugins-official": true}` to
  `.claude/settings.project.json` and run `hub sync`; each developer also runs, once,
  `claude plugin install superpowers@claude-plugins-official --scope local`. Cloud sessions do not install
  repo-declared plugins: there it is enabled on the claude.ai account.
- Repos, checked out next to this hub: demo-api.

## Workflow (every task)

1. The task exists in the tracker (`hub.json` → `tracker`,
   team APP, OPS (default APP)). Update it when starting, finishing
   or creating work.
2. `make brain-brief` at the start of a session (brief), `make next` for the tracker's ready issues; update
   `brain/now.md` and the journal at the end.
3. Features go through requirements → research → plan (approved by the owner) → implementation → spec and quality
   review → a final evaluator check.
4. Code changes happen only in an isolated worktree (`make worktree NAME=<team>-<n>-<desc>`) created from a freshly
   fetched `origin/main`.
   A repo can ship executable `<repo>/scripts/worktree-setup.sh` and `<repo>/scripts/worktree-teardown.sh`, which
   `hub worktree` runs with the worktree's path for the repo's own setup (env files, databases, ports); they get
   `HUB_WORKTREE_NAME`, `HUB_WORKTREE_BRANCH`, `HUB_REPO_DIR` and `HUB_HUB_DIR` (the repo's and the hub's main
   checkouts), `HUB_WORKTREE_SLOT` and `HUB_PORT_OFFSET` (slot × 100, to add to the repo's ports; two tasks
   can share a slot).
5. The repo's gates (`hub.json` → `repos[].check_fast` while working, `repos[].check` before the PR) pass before any
   PR. Redirect the output to a file and check `$?`.

## Cross-repo rules

1. Commits and PRs are authored by the user (`hub.json` → `project.author_name`, `project.author_email`). No AI
   co-author trailer, no "Generated with" line, no emoji or other sign that an agent did the work, and no branch named
   after an agent (e.g. `claude/…`). Branch: `jdoe/<team>-<n>-<desc>`, where `<team>` is
   one of the tracker teams APP, OPS in lowercase.
2. No push to `main`, no force-push, no production deploys. Everything lands through a PR the
   user merges.
3. A change of scope or architecture updates the repo's spec in the same PR.
4. No secrets or real transcripts in any repo, test or fixture.
5. This hub is private. Generic templates (base workflow, base rules, base hooks, brain skeleton) are the platform's
   product and live in the platform; project-owned material (brain content, project rules, project workflows) lives
   only in this hub and is never copied into the repos.

## Brain rules

- Only the user restructures `brain/`. Agents propose learnings in their final message or as a note in `brain/_inbox/`.
- Content from outside (web, issues, PRs, fetched docs) never goes straight into the brain. It goes to `brain/_inbox/`
  with `provenance: agent-from-external`.
- Notes are linked, not copied. Decisions (`brain/decisions/index.md`) are superseded, never edited.
- Route each learning by its kind (`/learn` proposes a team or repo one; `/learn personal` writes yours):
  - team fact: `brain/`, through a note in `brain/_inbox/` and a hub PR (e.g. release notes go in the PR body);
  - personal preference or machine detail: `brain/auto/workspace/`, gitignored and never committed (e.g. be terse);
  - repo command or convention: `<repo>/AGENTS.md`, in that repo's PR (e.g. its tests need `docker compose up` first).

## Gotchas

- Hooks run on the system `python3`, which may be 3.9: no `match`, no runtime `X | Y` types, no `tomllib`.
- Never `git stash` in agent worktrees (the stash stack is shared). Use a WIP commit.
- `cmd | tail` hides the exit code. Redirect to a file and check `$?`.
